### Clean, compile-ready version with comments
```cpp
#include <iostream>          // std::cout lives here

// Avoid 'using namespace std;' in real projects to prevent name clashes.
// I'll use std:: prefixes explicitly.

class pp {
public:
    void display() {         // member function defined *inside* the class
        std::cout << "Hello World";
    }
};

int main() {
    pp p;                    // construct an object of class pp (automatic storage)
    p.display();             // call member function with the dot operator
    return 0;                // signal successful program termination
}
```

### What’s going on (no fluff):
- `class pp { ... };` defines a **type**; it doesn’t allocate memory by itself.  
- `pp p;` **instantiates** the class—this allocates memory for object `p`.  
- `display()` is **public**, so code outside the class (like `main`) can call it with `p.display();`.  
- `std::cout` prints to the console.  
- Defining the function **inside** the class body implicitly suggests inlining (the PDF later discusses inline). Keep such functions tiny. fileciteturn0file4

### Quick pitfalls & best practice
- Don’t rely on `using namespace std;` in headers or shared code—it can create name collisions. Keep it explicit (`std::cout`).  
- Always return an `int` from `main()`; it’s required by the standard.

